  "id": "kalela_copperbelt",
  "name": "Kalela (Copperbelt mine-town team dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Team dance-song of the Northern Rhodesian Copperbelt mine towns in the 1950s, performed by troupes of Bisa migrants from the Luapula region, smartly dressed in European clothes and led by a 'king', singing about town life and their home district to drums. J. Clyde Mitchell analysed it in The Kalela Dance (Rhodes-Livingstone Papers 27, 1956), and Hugh Tracey recorded kalela on the Copperbelt for ILAM. The generic membrane-drum card stands in for the kalela drums.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1950s kalela, groove Percussion, central east south African branch. single leader group response. membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum. field recording. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
